    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The question of how large, multicultural democracies balance diversity and national identity is crucial in an age in which these countries are both continuously diversifying and witnessing a surge in xenophobic nationalism. The politics of data collection exists at the heart of this tension, as quantifying people based on race, ethnicity, or religion can imply government legitimation of social divisions. In the United States, counting citizens by race and ethnicity is extremely common, yet this same practice is forbidden by law in France. From the French perspective, ethnoracial census categories serve to advance group-oriented rhetoric and undermine a unified French national identity. The motivation for my research is to test this claim — does government collection of ethnoracial data have adverse effects on the coherence and strength of national identity? In addition to analyzing the history of ethnoracial data collection in the United States and France, I conduct a national survey experiment of Americans assessing how ethnoracial categorization impacts respondents’ feelings of national identity. Ultimately, I find that ethnoracial categorization influences the national identification of white and black Americans, an effect that I posit stems from a relationship between Americanness and whiteness.",
